# High-severity advisories this repository has accepted, one GHSA id per line.
#
# The daily bug hunt fails when `npm audit` reports a high advisory that is NOT in this
# file. It does not fail on the count, because a count over 1,487 dependencies is red
# forever and a check that is always red teaches people to ignore red. What a daily sweep
# is actually good at is noticing something NEW, so that is the only thing it gates on.
#
# Criticals are never listed here. They fail unconditionally, in their own step.
#
# Adding a line is a decision, and the diff is where it is reviewed: it says "we know, and
# we are shipping anyway". Removing one is free and the workflow tells you when it is safe,
# so the list shrinks as upstream releases land.
#
# Pruned 2026-08-20 from 21 entries to 3, after next 16.3.1 and sharp 0.35.3. 18 entries
# went stale in one bump, which is the case a count-based baseline would have passed in
# silence while leaving eighteen advisories of headroom for new ones to hide in.
#
# The three left are all resource-exhaustion (ReDoS or stack/CPU exhaustion) in build-side
# tooling, reached by our own toolchain on inputs we control rather than by a request.
#
# npm reports a fix as "available" for picomatch and path-to-regexp, and `npm audit fix`
# still changes nothing, including with --force. Both are pinned by a transitive parent that
# wants the vulnerable major: micromatch wants picomatch@^2.3.1 while everything else in the
# tree is already on 4.x, and router wants path-to-regexp@^8.0.0. Neither is reachable until
# those parents move. deepmerge-ts is honest about it: the only fix npm offers is a prisma
# downgrade to 6.12.0, flagged semver-major, via @prisma/config which pins deepmerge-ts@7.1.5
# exactly.
#
